---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro'; import { getCollection } from 'astro:content'; import { CATEGORIES } from '../../config/categories';
import { breadcrumbLd } from '../../lib/schema';
const guides = (await getCollection('guides')).sort((a,b)=>a.data.title.localeCompare(b.data.title));
const groups = CATEGORIES.map(c => ({ cat: c, guides: guides.filter(g => g.data.category === c.slug) })).filter(g => g.guides.length);
const jsonld = breadcrumbLd([{name:'Home',item:'https://thefaultcode.com/'},{name:'Guides',item:'https://thefaultcode.com/guides/'}]);
---
<Base title="Furnace, Washer, Dishwasher and AC Troubleshooting Guides | The Fault Code" description="Plain-English troubleshooting guides for furnace, washer, dishwasher and AC error codes: what the manuals say, which checks are safe, and when to call a technician." jsonld={jsonld}>
<div class="wrap">
<nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><ol><li><a href="/">Home</a></li><li aria-current="page">Guides</li></ol></nav>
<div class="page-h"><h1>Troubleshooting guides</h1><p>Answers to the questions homeowners ask most about a blinking furnace light, a washer that will not drain, a dishwasher leak code, or an AC that locks out on pressure. Every fact below is taken from a manufacturer manual or support page already linked on our code pages, or from a U.S. government safety or energy source. Nothing here is a guessed specification.</p></div>
<div class="card sec guide">
<p>These guides sit next to the code pages, not instead of them. A code page is the short record for one display reading: the manufacturer's meaning, the causes that document lists, the checks it allows a homeowner, and the point where it says to call a technician. A guide is the longer explanation of one problem that shows up under several brands, with a link to each of those code pages so you can open the source for your model.</p>
<p>Start with the characters on the display, or the flash pattern, and write them down before you turn the unit off. Resetting a control often clears the light and, on some boards, the stored history. The <a href="/guides/how-to-read-furnace-led-flash-codes/">furnace flash-code guide</a> shows where Carrier, Goodman, Lennox, Rheem and Trane put that light. Washer and dishwasher codes are letters or letter-number pairs on the console; the same letters are not interchangeable across brands or even across appliances from one brand. LG OE on a washer is a drain error. LG OE on a dishwasher is also a drain error, but the filter and hose checks are the dishwasher's, not the washer's. Samsung 4C on a washer means water is not entering. Samsung 4C on a dishwasher is a water-supply error with that brand's own pressure and temperature ranges. Use the code page for the appliance in front of you.</p>
<p>Homeowner checks on this site are only the ones the manufacturer wrote down: a dirty filter, a kinked hose, a closed faucet, a load that is piled on one side, detergent that is not marked HE, a pump filter full of debris. Gas valves, heat exchangers, vent sizing, refrigerant charge, sealed-system work, control boards and line-voltage diagnosis stay with a licensed technician. If you smell gas, leave the building and call your gas utility from outside. Do not hunt for the furnace light first.</p>
<p>Where a guide mentions a part or supply a homeowner might buy, the shop button opens an Amazon search for that generic name using this site's Associates tag. The button does not mean the first result fits your model. Match the part to the label on the old part or to the manual. Links are marked sponsored. Read the <a href="/affiliate-disclosure/">affiliate disclosure</a>. A guide with no shop button is one where the manuals do not point at a homeowner part, usually because the next step is a repair visit.</p>
<p>The lists under each heading link the code pages those guides cite, the <a href="/parts/">parts and tools guides</a> for the same components, and the equipment hubs: <a href="/furnaces/">furnaces</a>, <a href="/washers/">washing machines</a>, <a href="/dishwashers/">dishwashers</a> and <a href="/ac/">AC and heat pumps</a>. Search every published code from <a href="/codes/">all codes</a>.</p>
<p>Furnace guides cover the light itself, a safe reset, a dirty filter, a flame sensor, a pressure switch, short cycling, cold air, the repair-or-replace question from the Department of Energy, and rollout codes next to what the CPSC says about carbon monoxide alarms. Washer guides cover a tub that will not drain, a tub that will not fill, suds, a door that will not lock, and an unbalanced load or a leak code. Dishwasher guides cover the same four jobs on that appliance: drain, fill, leak, and the temperature, detergent and limescale codes, including the disposal knockout and the brand-specific temperature numbers. AC guides cover high and low pressure lockouts, where the only homeowner checks are coils, filters and the thermostat setting, and the voltage codes, where the only homeowner check is a tripped breaker. If a step is not on the code page for your model, it is not a step this site is willing to add.</p>
</div>
{groups.map(({cat, guides: list}) => <section aria-labelledby={`g-${cat.slug}`} style="margin-top:28px"><h2 class="cat-h" id={`g-${cat.slug}`}>{cat.name}<small>{list.length} guides · <a href={cat.hub}>{cat.name} error codes</a></small></h2>
<div class="grid g3">{list.map((g,i)=><a class="card" data-reveal href={`/guides/${g.id}/`}><div class="tag">Guide {String(i+1).padStart(2,"0")}</div><h3 style="font-size:1.6rem;margin:6px 0">{g.data.title}</h3><p style="margin:0;color:var(--steel)">{g.data.description}</p></a>)}</div></section>)}
</div>
</Base>
